## 2. Authority and governance

- MWAA is an independent public body created in 1987 under the Metropolitan
  Washington Airports Act; it operates IAD and DCA under a long-term lease
  from the U.S. Department of Transportation. Federal ownership, local
  operation.
- Board of Directors: 17 seats. Virginia's governor appoints seven (confirmed
  by the Virginia General Assembly); D.C.'s mayor appoints four (confirmed by
  D.C. Council); Maryland's governor appoints three; the President appoints
  three with U.S. Senate advice and consent. Members serve six-year terms and
  may be reappointed once. Officers are elected annually.
- 2026 officers: Mark Uncapher, chair; Alex Vogel, vice chair; Felice C. Smith,
  secretary (re-elected).
- Rulemaking and policy for the two airports flow through board resolutions
  and MWAA's published Orders and Instructions for IAD and DCA respectively.
  The Airports Authority Design Manual (seven volumes; IAD Vol. 1 covers
  airport design and signing, Vol. 2 the Main Terminal / Concourse Z tenant
  standards, Vol. 3 Concourse B tenant standards, and DCA has parallel
  volumes) is the natural home for a quiet-by-design standard, but the public
  record does not currently disclose explicit acoustic performance
  requirements (NC, RT60, STI, adjacent-zone spillover) inside those volumes.

## 3. Finance and credit

- New 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease with the signatory
  airlines effective January 1, 2025, replacing the prior agreement that
  expired December 31, 2024. Structured as a two-airport residual system
  designed to address the historical Reagan/Dulles cost imbalance.
- Attached to the new agreement: a $9.0 billion Capital Construction Program
  (CCP), split roughly $6.99 billion at IAD and $2.39 billion at DCA. The
  agreement includes a 140% airline-funded debt-service coverage target.
- 2025 debt issuance: $714.4 million Airport System Revenue and Refunding
  Bonds, Series 2025A. MWAA has publicly signalled roughly $5.5 billion of
  new debt across 2025–2028, with adjusted debt per O&D enplanement forecast
  to peak near $400 in 2028.
- Credit ratings (as of May 2025 affirmations): Moody's Aa3, S&P AA-, Fitch
  AA-; all three outlooks stable.
- Airline cost per enplanement: reported at $12.77 currently, with the 2025A
  official statement forecast rising to $20.71 by 2030 under the CCP.
- In late July 2026, MWAA, United Airlines, and USDOT jointly announced a
  larger vision: a $20 billion–$22.5 billion multi-decade Dulles
  transformation that would replace Concourses C and D, extend the AeroTrain,
  retire the mobile lounges, and touch more than 5 million square feet.
  Financing structure for the incremental scope beyond the existing $7 billion
  IAD CCP has not been publicly detailed and is being actively questioned in
  the trade press.

## 4. Airline framework

- Signatory-airline agreement is the 15-year Use & Lease effective 2025.
  Publicly available terms include the residual two-airport model, the $9 B
  CCP, and the 140% coverage target. Majority-in-interest (MII) thresholds
  governing airline concurrence on capital projects are conventional in
  residual agreements of this type, but the specific MII language, project
  categories, and any carve-outs for design manual changes or
  passenger-experience investments have not been disclosed publicly for this
  agreement and should not be assumed from industry convention.
- Anchor tenants and gate share:
  - **IAD:** United Airlines is the dominant carrier and signed a long-term
    lease at Dulles alongside the 2025 Use & Lease. Concourse E, opening
    later in 2026 (435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, ~46,000 sq ft of
    concessions, a 40,000-sq-ft United club, direct AeroTrain access), is
    delivered under this partnership.
  - **DCA:** American Airlines is the dominant carrier; Delta, United,
    Southwest, Alaska, JetBlue, and Air Canada Express also operate.
- Slot and perimeter rules at DCA remain governed by the FAA's High Density
  Rule and the statutory perimeter, most recently modified by Section 502 of
  the 2024 FAA Reauthorization, which authorized 10 additional daily beyond-
  perimeter slot exemptions. In December 2024 DOT awarded two exemptions each
  to Alaska (SAN), American (SAT), Delta (SEA), Southwest (LAS), and United
  (SFO); service was required to begin by March 17, 2025. Slot economics
  amplify the operational cost of a missed boarding at DCA more than at a
  typical hub, which is directly relevant to any quiet-terminal standard
  that reshapes how boarding is called.

## 5. Terminals, capital program, and physical context

- **IAD (Dulles):**
  - Landmark Eero Saarinen Main Terminal (1962), a designated architectural
    icon whose formal preservation is stated policy under the modernization
    program. Midfield concourses A/B (regional and international) and C/D
    (older United piers) currently connected by AeroTrain and the residual
    mobile lounges.
  - Concourse E (14 United gates) is the first delivered piece of the
    modernization and is scheduled to open later in 2026.
  - The July 2026 announcement calls for replacing Concourses C and D,
    extending AeroTrain, adding a new central walking tunnel and moving
    walkways, retiring the mobile lounges, and creating or renovating more
    than 5 million square feet over the coming decade. The scope will
    generate a long pipeline of new terminal space where a quiet-by-design
    standard could be applied before design and procurement harden.
- **DCA (Reagan National):**
  - Terminal 1 (gates A1–A9): the original 1941 art deco terminal;
    Southwest-dominant with Frontier and Air Canada Express.
  - Terminal 2 (concourses B, C, D, E; gates B10–E59): the 1997 Cesar Pelli
    National Hall with attached concourses; serves American, Delta, United,
    Alaska, and JetBlue.
  - The two terminals are not connected post-security. DCA recently
    standardized gate/terminal signage terminology to match industry norms.
  - Under the current CCP, DCA receives roughly $2.4 billion of investment,
    focused on gate area capacity, curb/roadway, and terminal repairs rather
    than a wholesale rebuild.

## 6. Traffic and operating conditions (calendar year 2025)

- Combined system: just under 54 million passengers — a system record for
  the two-airport authority.
- IAD: approximately 29 million total passengers and 10.5 million
  international passengers, both all-time highs.
- DCA: approximately 24.9 million passengers, down 5.3% from 26.3 million in
  2024 and the lowest annual total since 2022. Three drivers cited publicly:
  (a) the January 2025 midair collision between an Army helicopter and a
  regional jet on approach to DCA and the flight restrictions that followed;
  (b) a seven-week federal government shutdown in fall 2025; (c) softer
  federal-government-related travel demand tied to workforce reductions.
- These traffic conditions matter for the quiet-terminal argument in two
  ways: DCA's short average curb-to-gate journey and heavy business/federal
  mix drive different PA and information-density needs than IAD's much longer
  international transfer journeys, and DCA's operational fragility to
  irregular ops (single main runway, slot constraints, incident-driven
  restrictions) sets the floor on how minimal announcement policy can go.

## 7. Regulatory, safety, and accessibility constraints

- FAA design and operational standards (Part 139, NFPA 415, TSA screening
  layouts, and CBP FIS requirements at IAD) constrain terminal layout, egress,
  and public-information design. Any quiet standard must be compatible with
  emergency notification, TSA queue communication, and CBP arrival messaging.
- Emergency notification requirements: NFPA 72 governs voice evacuation
  systems and intelligibility; ADA and Section 504 govern accessible
  information; ACAA (Air Carrier Access Act) governs airline-provided
  communication in the terminal. A quiet-by-design standard has to specify
  compliance envelopes for each, not silence them.
- MWAA operates under a federal lease from USDOT and receives FAA AIP and
  BIL/IIJA grant funds; grant assurances constrain procurement and asset
  disposition. Board resolutions and federal grant assurances therefore
  govern how a new design standard can be embedded in consultant scopes and
  contractor specifications.
- The current federal administration is a visible political stakeholder in
  the Dulles program; the July 2026 announcement was made jointly with the
  White House and the Secretary of Transportation. Any high-visibility change
  to passenger-facing standards at IAD will be interpreted in that political
  context.

## 8. Named gaps in the public record

The following facts would meaningfully sharpen the analysis but are not in
the public record reviewed for this packet. The research swarm should not
guess at them; if they turn out to matter, they should be surfaced as open
questions in the final deliverable.

1. Whether the current MWAA Airports Authority Design Manual contains
   explicit acoustic performance criteria (target NC/RC levels, T60/RT30
   ranges by space type, adjacent-zone STI or Speech Transmission Index
   thresholds, PA loudspeaker density and coverage overlap requirements).
2. Whether either airport currently has a written PA governance policy
   (announcement categories permitted, scripting, frequency caps, zoning
   rules, quiet-hours, tenant broadcast rights).
3. The specific MII thresholds, categories, and airline-review triggers in
   the 2025 Use & Lease as they apply to design standards, PA/FIDS systems,
   and passenger-experience capital.
4. Whether MWAA has piloted quiet-terminal, silent-boarding, or reduced-
   announcement practices at any concourse or hold-room in the last decade,
   and any measured results.
5. Current employee sound-exposure monitoring practice for gate agents,
   concessions staff, and airport operations personnel in the noisiest
   hold-room and concourse zones at both airports.
6. Financing structure for the incremental Dulles scope announced in July
   2026 beyond the $7 B IAD CCP already inside the 2025 Use & Lease, and
   whether airline concurrence for that incremental scope is complete.

